{"name":"Honky Tonk Blue","hex":"#446a8d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A white (Caucasian) person.","A factory hand or general unskilled worker."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honky"},{"term":"tonk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An illegal immigrant of any country."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An item or items of value, or of perceived value, especially for sale."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tonk"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honky-tonk","blocks":[{"text":"The term honky-tonk refers either to a bar that provides country music for the entertainment of its patrons, or to the particular genre of music which is played at such establishments itself. It can also refer to the type of piano used to play such music. Bars of this kind are common in the American South and Southwest. Many prominent country music artists such as Jimmie Rodgers, Ernest Tubb, Lefty Frizzell, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Johnny Horton and Merle Haggard began their careers as amateur musicians in honky-tonks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Tootsies_2015.jpg/330px-Tootsies_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honky-tonk","wikidata":"Q1627155"},{"title":"Honky","blocks":[{"text":"Honky is a derogatory term used to refer to white people, predominantly heard in the United States. The first recorded use of \"honky\" in this context may date back to 1946."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honky","wikidata":"Q5896426"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/MerleHaggardJun09.jpg/500px-MerleHaggardJun09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/MerleHaggardJun09.jpg/1280px-MerleHaggardJun09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/MerleHaggardJun09.jpg/500px-MerleHaggardJun09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Merle Haggard, an American country music songwriter, singer, and musician (1937–2016)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMerleHaggardJun09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Johnny_Paycheck.jpg/330px-Johnny_Paycheck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c